WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.930
You hold yourself back from laughing at the man who slips and falls in the street — yet if he gets up and laughs at his own fall, you join in without a second thought.

2
00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:14.660
One and the same sound in two states: one of them crushes a person, the other rescues them.

3
00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.950
So when you laugh, how do you know which one you are doing?

4
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.840
A man is running down the street.

5
00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:35.410
He is in a hurry; maybe he is trying to catch a bus, maybe he is very late for someone.

6
00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:43.700
His foot catches on a raised edge at the side of the pavement, his balance goes, his arms wheel through the air once or twice, and he falls.

7
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.550
The passers-by laugh.

8
00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:54.840
This scene sits in the opening pages of a book the French philosopher Henri Bergson published in nineteen hundred, Le Rire, or Laughter.

9
00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:59.450
Bergson puts the scene down not as an example of a joke but as a riddle.

10
00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:02.140
Because there is nothing funny here.

11
00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:08.190
The man played no trick on anyone, deceived no one, made no witty remark about himself.

12
00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.240
He simply fell.

13
00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:16.930
And those same people, if they saw that his knee was bleeding, would stop laughing within a second and rush to help.

14
00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:20.260
So the ones laughing are not bad people.

15
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:22.950
But they are laughing.

16
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:27.880
Now look at that pavement from the other side, from the side of the people laughing.

17
00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:30.250
They did not decide to laugh.

18
00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:34.700
There was no time to decide; the sound came out before any decision.

19
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:40.430
In this respect laughter resembles a sneeze — involuntary, lodged in the body.

20
00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:45.360
But here is the strange part: nobody is ashamed of sneezing.

21
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.370
Laughter, we are ashamed of.

22
00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:53.140
If that man stood up and looked the laughers in the eye, most of them would look away.

23
00:01:53.140 --> 00:02:05.430
None of us says, "I sneezed, forgive me," yet all of us know how to say, "sorry, I laughed." So laughter is both a reaction outside our control and a piece of conduct we can be held to account for.

24
00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:09.400
The only reflex a human being apologises for.

25
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:13.370
Modern research has followed the same double nature.

26
00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:25.820
The American neuroscientist Robert Provine noted down when people laughed — on the street, in shopping centres, in university corridors — and once he had recorded more than twelve hundred moments of laughter,

27
00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:28.030
two things emerged.

28
00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:35.360
The first was that a person alone almost never laughs; laughter multiplies many times over when someone else is present.

29
00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:41.890
The second, and the more surprising one, was that most of that laughter had not been preceded by any joke at all.

30
00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:54.660
The overwhelming majority of the lines that triggered laughter were of this kind: "Where have you been?" "That's what I thought too." "All right, see you." So laughter is not, in the main, an answer given to jokes.

31
00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.150
It is a signal sprinkled through conversation.

32
00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:01.320
A kind of punctuation.

33
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.650
And a signal always has someone it is addressed to.

34
00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:08.460
This is exactly where the whole question begins.

35
00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:11.310
Laughter has a direction.

36
00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:19.280
Language has buried the distinction in its grammar, and we use it every day without noticing: laughing at someone, and laughing with them.

37
00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.290
The sound is the same.

38
00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:28.980
The same muscle tightens, the same breath catches, even the number of decibels in the room is the same.

39
00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:33.750
But one of them lays that person out on the table, and the other seats them at it.

40
00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:39.960
You do not need an instrument to measure this, because the person on the receiving end knows instantly.

41
00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:47.130
A young newcomer in a meeting mispronounces a foreign word during a presentation and there is a short ripple of giggling in the room.

42
00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:53.180
Within half a second you can watch a calculation cross that young person's face: is this aimed at me?

43
00:03:53.180 --> 00:04:04.990
If someone at the table then says, "I read that word wrong for years myself," and puts their own mistake on display, the very same giggling becomes shared, the direction flips, and the newcomer relaxes.

44
00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:10.560
Nothing, in fact, has been said; only the address of the laughter has changed.

45
00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.410
The same thing happens at a family dinner.

46
00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:22.980
The difference between laughter that gathers around a condition, an accent, a body weight, a failure, and laughter in which everyone is caught by the same absurdity,

47
00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:26.630
shows in who gets up from the table with a knot in their chest.

48
00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:31.240
So the question is this: where exactly does that line run?

49
00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:35.210
Why is laughter sometimes a gift and sometimes a slap?

50
00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:42.060
And who draws the distinction — the one who makes the joke, the one who laughs, or the one who is laughed at?

51
00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:47.630
The first sharp answer the history of philosophy gave to this question is not at all comforting.

52
00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:59.280
The English philosopher Thomas Hobbes, in his great work Leviathan, published in sixteen fifty-one, and in a text on human nature he had written eleven years earlier, gives laughter a very cold definition:

53
00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:02.130
laughter is sudden glory.

54
00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:08.980
For Hobbes, what pulls our faces into that odd shape is the sudden recognition of some superiority in ourselves.

55
00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:19.350
Either we have just managed something we did not expect to manage, or — far more often — we have spotted in somebody else a flaw, an incapacity, a crookedness,

56
00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:23.960
and by comparison with that crookedness we have abruptly applauded ourselves.

57
00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:29.530
The laughter on the pavement, by this definition, is not even about the man who fell.

58
00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:31.740
It is about the people laughing.

59
00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:36.990
For half a second, each of them said to themselves: I am still standing.

60
00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.680
Hobbes does not write this as praise.

61
00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:44.130
On the contrary, he counts laughter as a sign of weakness.

62
00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:54.980
In his view the person of great spirit measures themselves not against other people's deficiencies but against the best; the man who is forever laughing is the man who can only gather his standing by shrinking others.

63
00:05:54.980 --> 00:06:05.510
It is worth remembering that the person writing this lived in the middle of a civil war, describing a world in which everyone was appraising everyone else, where rank and respect were renegotiated daily.

64
00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:10.280
And it has to be admitted: the definition does not stay locked in a dusty book.

65
00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:19.690
A photograph dropped into a workplace group chat, the endless running joke built on somebody's accent, the impression performed in the lift on the way out of a meeting.

66
00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:25.580
None of these is quite a joke; each is a ledger of superiority, kept in small entries.

67
00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:32.270
Bergson approaches the same phenomenon from somewhere else entirely, and his conclusion again points downward.

68
00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:37.200
For him laughter has a single precondition: the heart must go numb for a moment.

69
00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:40.370
You cannot laugh at someone whose pain you feel.

70
00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:44.820
The instant any pity enters the scene, comedy collapses.

71
00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:49.750
Laughter, then, is the product not of affection but of a brief anaesthesia.

72
00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:52.280
And what is it for?

73
00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:57.530
Bergson's answer is that laughter is a social gesture, an instrument of correction.

74
00:06:57.530 --> 00:06:59.580
Correcting what?

75
00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:01.310
Rigidity.

76
00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:05.280
His famous formula is the mechanical stuck onto the living.

77
00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:15.970
Anything that insists on behaving like a machine where life is asking for flexibility is comic: the official who applies the rule to the letter although the situation has changed,

78
00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:24.420
the man still speaking in the sentences he used thirty years ago, the person walking along absent-mindedly who has not noticed that the road has turned.

79
00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:28.390
Society notices that rigidity and punishes it.

80
00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:32.680
The punishment is not prison; it is a small humiliation.

81
00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:38.890
Bergson says so plainly: to correct, laughter has to shame, otherwise it does not work.

82
00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:43.660
Two philosophers, two different centuries, two different worlds.

83
00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:48.910
Yet both seem to be saying the same thing: the arrow of laughter points downward.

84
00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:52.400
For Hobbes, I am laughing for my own sake.

85
00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:57.330
For Bergson, society is laughing, using my mouth to do it.

86
00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:04.020
In both, laughter has a victim; in one, fuel for my pride, in the other, the stick of discipline.

87
00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:10.070
The trouble is that both of these answers struggle to explain the most common form laughter takes in life.

88
00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:14.040
Because human beings also laugh at themselves.

89
00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:21.690
In a conversation where nobody has fallen and nobody has been diminished, two friends can laugh for half an hour until their eyes water.

90
00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:28.060
And the funniest people in most of our lives are not the ones who belittle us; they are the ones who include us.

91
00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:35.070
Freud noticed this crack in the account in the book he wrote on jokes in nineteen oh five, and he divided the field in two.

92
00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:44.640
On one side are jokes that really do have a target, smuggling hostility or desire past a prohibition and out into the open; this is the territory Hobbes described.

93
00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:56.290
On the other side are jokes with no target at all, running purely on the pleasure of language, of sound, of resemblance, of an unexpected connection; in these there is no person being laughed at,

94
00:08:56.290 --> 00:08:59.620
and what is laughed at is the play itself.

95
00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:02.790
So the issue is not where laughter comes from.

96
00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:05.160
The issue is measure.

97
00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:11.690
And to look for measure, we have to go to a tradition of thought that does not condemn laughter but has drawn its boundary.

98
00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:16.940
When Aristotle defines comedy in the Poetics, he adds a very fine qualification.

99
00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:26.830
Comedy, he says, shows people as somewhat worse than they are; but the laughable is a particular species of the ugly — a flaw that causes no pain and does no damage.

100
00:09:26.830 --> 00:09:31.760
That single sentence carries an entire ethical criterion inside it.

101
00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:35.090
The boundary of the comic is pain.

102
00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:40.660
The moment a flaw begins to hurt, it leaves comedy and turns into something else.

103
00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:46.230
Aristotle is not forbidding laughter here; he is stating laughter's own nature.

104
00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:50.840
A joke that wounds is not merely a bad joke; it is no longer a joke.

105
00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:55.930
In the Nicomachean Ethics he carries the same idea up to the level of character.

106
00:09:55.930 --> 00:10:01.340
Aristotle's virtues always sit in the middle between two excesses, and wit is one of them.

107
00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:11.070
At one extreme stands the buffoon: the person who will say anything for a laugh, who spares no one and nothing, who will sell the person beside them for one burst of laughter.

108
00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:18.400
At the other extreme stands the boor, who can neither make a joke nor take one, who meets every witticism with his own solemnity.

109
00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:25.730
The person Aristotle praises is in the middle, and their real skill is not making people laugh; it is knowing what not to say.

110
00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:28.740
This is a strikingly modern idea.

111
00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:35.910
The measure of intelligence is not delivering the joke that occurs to you, but leaving some part of the joke that occurs to you unsaid.

112
00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:43.720
The same criterion is set down in a completely different language by the chapter of the Quran called Al-Hujurat, the Rooms.

113
00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:52.170
Its eleventh verse forbids one community from mocking another; it also forbids the giving of insulting nicknames and the finding of fault with one another.

114
00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:55.020
What is remarkable here is the reason given.

115
00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:58.510
The reason is not that mockery is coarse behaviour.

116
00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:02.800
The reason has to do with knowledge: perhaps they are better than you.

117
00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:09.170
In other words, the person mocking is making use of information they do not actually possess.

118
00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:16.340
They are drawing up a ranking and placing themselves at the top, when they have no instrument whatever for verifying that ranking.

119
00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:24.470
What Hobbes called sudden glory is here diagnosed precisely and rejected: a verdict without evidence, hidden inside a laugh.

120
00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:34.520
The next verse of the same chapter places talking behind people's backs in the same family, because both run on the same fuel — the reputation of someone who is not in the room.

121
00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:39.450
One of those who worked this line out in the greatest detail was al-Ghazali.

122
00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:51.260
This thinker, who lived in the eleventh century, listed the afflictions of the tongue one by one in his great work Ihya Ulum al-Din, The Revival of the Religious Sciences, and he put humour on that list too.

123
00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:56.670
But where he put it matters: he does not forbid humour itself, because he cannot.

124
00:11:56.670 --> 00:12:01.600
The sources contain accounts of the Prophet joking, and al-Ghazali knows them.

125
00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:15.490
A small boy's bird has died, and he teases him: "O Abu Umayr, what has the little bird done?" To a man who asks for a mount he says, "I shall put you on the offspring of a she-camel"; when the man objects in confusion,

126
00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:19.620
he reminds him that every camel is the offspring of a she-camel.

127
00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:28.710
The point al-Ghazali underlines is this: in none of these jokes is there a falsehood, and in none of them is the dignity of the person addressed paid out as the price.

128
00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:35.720
The child is not made small, the man is not made small; both of them are partners in the joke rather than its audience.

129
00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:39.050
Al-Ghazali's warning runs in two directions.

130
00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:48.940
The first concerns habit: joking that becomes constant erodes a person's capacity for seriousness, and after a while you find yourself unable to carry anything.

131
00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:52.910
The second, and the sharper one, concerns mockery.

132
00:12:52.910 --> 00:13:03.440
When al-Ghazali describes mockery he puts technique at the centre of the definition: imitating someone's words, their condition, their walk or their shortcoming so that others will laugh,

133
00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:06.930
and doing it in a way that makes that person smaller.

134
00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:12.020
Here the laughter is not a by-product; the target's shame is the fuel itself.

135
00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:17.750
And so the line we were looking for runs right through here, and it is less blurred than people assume.

136
00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:22.200
Wit looks at a situation; mockery looks at a person.

137
00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:30.650
Wit takes aim at the contradiction, the oddity, at language itself; mockery takes aim at the person who cannot get out of that contradiction.

138
00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:34.300
In everyday life you can test it with three questions.

139
00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:37.470
Is the person being spoken about here?

140
00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:40.800
If they were here, could they join in this laughter?

141
00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:44.290
And most importantly: who is paying for this laughter?

142
00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:47.780
If everyone leaves the table lighter, it was wit.

143
00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:51.910
If one person leaves heavier, it goes by another name.

144
00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:56.840
One further caution is needed, because this is where we go wrong most often.

145
00:13:56.840 --> 00:14:00.330
The person being laughed at very often laughs too.

146
00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:06.380
But that laughter is not always consent; sometimes it is simply a survival tactic.

147
00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:13.870
The weakest person in the room learns to join in the laughter that is mocking them, because not joining in means admitting you are the target.

148
00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:18.800
This is why the sentence "look, even they're laughing" proves nothing at all.

149
00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:24.690
To know whether a laugh is voluntary or compulsory, you have to look at how power is distributed in that room.

150
00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:30.260
And the moment we start talking about power, the story takes on an entirely different dimension.

151
00:14:30.260 --> 00:14:38.390
Because throughout history the people most afraid of jokes have not been the ordinary people being laughed at; they have been the ones sitting on thrones.

152
00:14:38.390 --> 00:14:47.320
Nasreddin Hodja lived in the thirteenth century; by tradition he died in Akşehir, and the date on his gravestone corresponds to the year twelve eighty-four.

153
00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:50.490
Timur was born in thirteen thirty-six.

154
00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:58.140
Which means the Hodja died more than half a century before the ruler he sits across from and trades lines with in the stories was even born.

155
00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:01.310
This is not an error; it is a need.

156
00:15:01.310 --> 00:15:10.880
The people added those stories later, because they needed a man who would not drop his gaze in front of the most feared figure of the age, and who answered him with wit rather than a sword.

157
00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:15.170
That is how Nasreddin stopped being a person and became a method.

158
00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:24.900
And the finest detail of that method is this: in most of the stories the Hodja is talking about his own stinginess, his own laziness, his own cunning.

159
00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:29.030
He earns the right to mock the sultan by mocking himself first.

160
00:15:29.030 --> 00:15:33.480
The mirror he turns upward, he has first held to his own face.

161
00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:37.290
This permission has had its institutional forms as well.

162
00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:45.420
On the Karagöz shadow screen, from the sixteenth century onward according to tradition, the men of the state, the tricks of the tradesmen,

163
00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:53.870
the hypocrisy of the neighbourhood were openly imitated — but the one doing the imitating was not a human being; it was the shadow of a piece of leather.

164
00:15:53.870 --> 00:15:59.600
The screen granted both the licence to speak and the right to escape responsibility.

165
00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:11.890
There is an older case too: the Roman historian Suetonius reports that at Caesar's triumph the soldiers sang songs mocking their own commander, and even shouted coarse verses advising the public to hide their wives.

166
00:16:11.890 --> 00:16:18.740
The empire allowed itself to be mocked on one day of the year, because it sensed that allowing nothing at all would cost more.

167
00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:23.350
What happens when the permission narrows is shown by newspaper archives.

168
00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:31.800
In the Ottoman world the first great address of humour was Diyojen, meaning Diogenes, brought out by Teodor Kasap in eighteen seventy.

169
00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:38.330
In its short life the magazine was shut down repeatedly, and in eighteen seventy-three it was silenced altogether.

170
00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:50.780
Kasap started new magazines, and in eighteen seventy-seven a cartoon published in one called Hayal, meaning Fantasy, became famous: Karagöz stands there in chains, and when asked whether the press is free,

171
00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:56.830
his answer is that liberty is free within the frame of the constitution, the Kanun-ı Esasi.

172
00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:58.880
One sentence.

173
00:16:58.880 --> 00:17:03.810
The penalty handed down for it was, according to the sources, three years in prison.

174
00:17:03.810 --> 00:17:09.220
A drawing of a chain and a joke ten words long had obliged a state to convene a court.

175
00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:11.750
There are darker examples.

176
00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:21.320
In nineteen thirty-four Nazi Germany brought in the regulation later known as the Malicious Practices Act, and jokes told about the regime became a formal matter for prosecution.

177
00:17:21.320 --> 00:17:32.010
In one of the cases tried under these laws, a priest named Josef Müller was prosecuted over a single joke he had told about a dying soldier, and he was executed in nineteen forty-four.

178
00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:39.500
When a state kills someone over a joke, it is announcing its own conviction about the power of jokes in the clearest possible terms.

179
00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:42.030
So where does this fear come from?

180
00:17:42.030 --> 00:17:45.520
The answer lies in the fuel that power runs on.

181
00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:49.010
Power moves by way of seriousness.

182
00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:56.980
Against criticism it has instruments: it can produce a counter-argument, publish documents, punish if need be.

183
00:17:56.980 --> 00:18:00.150
But laughter does not argue.

184
00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:05.240
Laughter does not refute the other side's claim; it drains the claim of its weight.

185
00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:14.970
In the Rhetoric, Aristotle passes on a tactic attributed to Gorgias: break your opponent's seriousness with laughter, and their laughter with seriousness.

186
00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:17.340
Why this works is plain.

187
00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:25.950
A power that has lost its seriousness still has force in its hands, but obedience is gone; it goes on being feared, and it is not respected.

188
00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:31.200
Today the same mechanism is cheaper and faster than it has ever been in its history.

189
00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:44.610
Making a meme requires no printing press, no licence, no capital, no distribution network; a phone is enough, and an image reaches, in half a day, places no editorial column could ever get to.

190
00:18:44.610 --> 00:18:48.580
In the history of laughter that looks upward, this is an expansion.

191
00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:53.350
But the same cheapness has also made it easier to reverse the arrow.

192
00:18:53.350 --> 00:19:04.520
A few seconds of a stranger having a bad day in the street are recorded and dropped onto millions of screens, and not one of those millions knows that person's name, their story, what happened to them that day.

193
00:19:04.520 --> 00:19:14.410
What Bergson set down as the precondition of laughter, the heart going numb for a moment, is now supplied automatically: the screen distributes that numbness free of charge.

194
00:19:14.410 --> 00:19:19.340
The people who saw the man fall on the pavement could at least see his bleeding knee.

195
00:19:19.340 --> 00:19:23.150
On the far side of a screen there is no such possibility.

196
00:19:23.150 --> 00:19:29.840
This is why the answer to the question we began with does not lie, as people assume, in the content of the joke.

197
00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:36.050
You cannot say with certainty, by looking at the words of a sentence, whether it is wit or mockery.

198
00:19:36.050 --> 00:19:40.180
The answer lies in how freedom is distributed around that laughter.

199
00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:45.430
What happens in a room where laughing is compulsory is not humour; it is a salute.

200
00:19:45.430 --> 00:19:51.480
What happens in a room where staying silent is permitted is humour, because there the laugh is saying something.

201
00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:57.370
And perhaps the soundest criterion is this: does the person being laughed at have the right to reply?

202
00:19:57.370 --> 00:20:04.380
Mockery is built on leaving the other person mute; the target's only options are to keep quiet or to be the one who spoils the fun.

203
00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:07.710
Wit always leaves the other person a move to make.

204
00:20:07.710 --> 00:20:15.680
Nasreddin Hodja's real genius was not that he humiliated the sultan; it was that he always left the sultan an answer he could give.

205
00:20:15.680 --> 00:20:23.170
At the dinner table, in the classroom, at work, on a screen, this is the quickest way to tell which side we are on.

206
00:20:23.170 --> 00:20:28.900
After the laughter, is there a silence, or is a conversation beginning?

207
00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:30.630
